## RFIG Analysis — Soon Thian Keing Temple / Shuntian Gong (顺天宫)
### Geylang Lane 29, Singapore

---

### Institutional identity

```
Full name:       顺天宫 (Shùn Tiān Gōng)
Hokkien:         Soon Thian Keing (顺天宫)
                 — the name by which it is known in
                   Singapore's Hokkien community

Components:
  顺  (Shùn)  — Following / Harmonious with / Compliant
  天  (Tiān)  — Heaven / Cosmic Order
  宫  (Gōng)  — Palace / Temple (高 high-status designation)

Full translation:
  "Palace of Heavenly Harmony" /
  "Temple of Following Heaven"

Naming convention: ASPIRATIONAL
  — This is the corpus's first aspirational temple name.
    Previous conventions documented:
    · Deity-named: 観音堂, 三圣殿, 觀音殿
    · Virtue-attributed: 福德祠 (Fu De = Tu Di Gong's virtue)
    · Locality: 沙岡萬山福德祠
    · Doctrinal: 光明殿, 無相殿
    Here the name encodes a cosmic value (天 heaven) and
    an aspirational orientation (顺 harmony with it) as
    the institution's identity claim.

Founding date: 1821 (original site, Balaba Street)
Current site:  Geylang Lane 29 (since 1994)
```

**Institutional history:** Shuntian Temple was founded in 1821, originally located on Balaba Street, commonly known as "White Sand Float" (Robanjan). In 1983, the government acquired the original site of Shuntian Temple. The temple then relocated, eventually settling at its current location in Geylang Lane 29. With over 200 years of continuous operation, Soon Thian Keing is among Singapore's oldest Chinese religious institutions — substantially older than any temple previously documented in this corpus, including LSSL (founded 1898) and KMSPKS (founded 1921).

**Identification note:** This is Li Ying's own fieldwork site for the EASSSR paper examining Chinese Mahayana Buddhism sustaining Taoist and folk religion temples. The RFIG analysis of this temple directly contributes to that paper's empirical foundation — the presence of 观音佛祖 (Guan Yin as Ancestor Buddha) in the back hall of a temple whose principal identity is Taoist/folk demonstrates precisely the Buddhist-folk dynamic the paper investigates. This is also the Soon Thian Keing Temple mentioned in the earlier Zhao Cai Tong Zi fieldwork discussions.

**Institutional type:** HOKKIEN-ASSOCIATED FOLK TEMPLE, Tua Pek Kong (大伯公/福德正神) as principal deity. The 宫 designation signals high institutional prestige — the same designation used in the more elaborate multi-faith Loyang Tua Pek Kong, distinguishing both from the 庙 (folk temple) and 祠 (shrine) designations.

