**Who are these figures:**

```
太岁 (Tài Suì):
  Sixty annual fate generals governing fortune through
  the sixty-year cycle. Already documented at Mun San
  Fook Tuck Chee, Loyang Tua Pek Kong.

包公 (Bāo Gōng, Judge Bao):
  The Song-dynasty magistrate Bao Zheng (999-1062),
  deified as the incorruptible judge of both human
  and divine law. Recognizable by his dark/black face.
  The archetypal figure of divine justice in Chinese
  folk religion. FIRST APPEARANCE in this corpus.

财神爷 (Cái Shén Yé):
  God of Wealth. Yang-realm variant (the living world
  wealth deity) — contrasted with 阴财神 (underworld
  wealth deity) documented at Loyang and Mun San.
  FIRST EXPLICIT YANG WEALTH DEITY documented in corpus.

孔子 (Kǒng Zǐ, Confucius):
  The philosopher venerated as the patron of education,
  scholarship, and moral cultivation. His presence in a
  folk temple marks the incorporation of the Confucian
  tradition into the Chinese folk religious pantheon.
  FIRST APPEARANCE in this corpus.

法主圣君 (Fǎ Zhǔ Shèng Jūn):
  The Holy Lord of the Law — a powerful regional deity
  of southern China associated with legal authority,
  moral judgment, and control of demonic forces. Often
  depicted with wild hair and an intense, fierce
  expression. Particularly venerated in Hokkien and
  Fujian communities. FIRST APPEARANCE in this corpus.

济公活佛 (Jì Gōng Huó Fó, Living Buddha Ji Gong):
  The eccentric Chan Buddhist monk Daoji (1130-1209),
  deified as a "Living Buddha" — known for his
  miraculous healing powers, his disregard for rules
  (breaking vegetarian precepts, drinking wine), and
  his compassion for the poor. Represents the populist
  Buddhist healing tradition. FIRST APPEARANCE in corpus.
  Note: Ji Gong as a Buddhist folk figure in a Taoist
  temple reinforces the Buddhist-folk syncretic pattern
  that the EASSSR paper investigates.

保生大帝 (Bǎo Shēng Dà Dì):
  Great Emperor Who Protects Life — the deified
  Song-dynasty physician Wu Tao (979-1036), patron
  of medicine and health. Particularly venerated by
  Hokkien communities. Previously documented at
  Loyang Tua Pek Kong.

二郎神 (Èr Láng Shén, Erlang Shen):
  The Three-Eyed Martial God — nephew of the Jade
  Emperor, divine warrior, cosmic law enforcer. Known
  for his third eye (天眼, Heavenly Eye) which sees
  all truth and evil. Associated with flood control,
  martial power, and divine surveillance. FIRST
  APPEARANCE in this corpus.
```

**Functional taxonomy of the right shrine assembly:**

```
JUDICIAL / LEGAL:
  包公 (incorruptible divine judge)
  法主圣君 (lord of supernatural law)
  二郎神 (cosmic law enforcer with all-seeing third eye)

COSMOLOGICAL / TEMPORAL:
  太岁 (annual fate governance)

COMMERCIAL / MATERIAL:
  财神爷 (yang-realm wealth)

EDUCATIONAL / MORAL:
  孔子 (scholarship, moral cultivation)

MEDICAL / HEALING:
  保生大帝 (patron of medicine)
  济公 (miraculous healing, Buddhist folk tradition)
```

The assembly encodes a **comprehensive civic welfare programme** — covering every dimension of community life that a citizen might need divine intercession in: justice (包公, 法主圣君, 二郎神), fate (太岁), wealth (财神爷), education (孔子), and health (保生大帝, 济公).

**The triadic judicial assembly:**

The three judicial deities (包公, 法主圣君, 二郎神) together form a judicial triad of complementary scopes:

```
包公:    Human law → divine law
         (incorruptible earthly judge extended to
          the divine realm)

法主圣君: Supernatural law enforcement
          (specifically against demonic and
           malevolent forces)

二郎神:  Cosmic surveillance and enforcement
          (all-seeing third eye, divine warrior)

Together: A complete judicial coverage from
          the human-legal to the supernatural-cosmic
          — no transgression can escape these three.
```

This connects to the 大二爷伯 couplet at the entrance — "the net of divine justice is hard to escape" — now substantiated by a full judicial triad within the main hall. The compound's theological emphasis on divine justice is systematic, not incidental.

