**Mazu's presence is iconographically coherent:**

Mazu (媽祖) is the Goddess of the Sea, protector of sailors and maritime communities. The original Shuntian Temple was located on Balaba Street near the Singapore waterfront — serving a maritime and fishing community. Mazu's presence in the back hall connects to the temple's founding maritime community context, even as the temple has moved inland.

**The 佛祖 designation and the Buddhist-folk dynamic:**

The elevation of Guanyin to 佛祖 status in the back hall of a Tua Pek Kong temple is the clearest documentation in this corpus of the Buddhist-folk syncretic relationship Li Ying's EASSSR paper investigates:

```
FRONT HALL: Tua Pek Kong (福德正神)
  Taoist/folk institutional identity
  Community welfare, earth-governance, prosperity

BACK HALL: 观音佛祖 (Guan Yin as Ancestor Buddha)
  Buddhist institutional presence
  Soteriological depth, compassionate transcendence

THEOLOGICAL CLAIM:
  By placing Guanyin佛祖 in the rear sanctum —
  behind Tua Pek Kong — and by elevating her to
  佛祖 status, the temple encodes a hierarchical
  but complementary relationship:

  Tua Pek Kong governs the community's THIS-WORLD
