### Summary statement

Soon Thian Keing Temple (顺天宫), one of Singapore's oldest Chinese religious institutions (founded 1821), is a Tua Pek Kong folk temple at Geylang Lane 29 whose compound encodes the corpus's most justice-emphasizing institutional theology alongside a comprehensive civic welfare programme. The entrance deploys the corpus's second documented threshold-pair convention — Tiger God (left, ecomorphic cave format; first documented *frames[ecomorphic]* relation in the corpus) and 大二爷伯 (right, with "the net of divine justice is hard to escape" couplets) — providing complementary coverage of harmful-entry prevention and managed death-departure. The main front hall's three-bay programme addresses relational welfare (和合二仙, left), earth-community welfare (Tua Pek Kong, center), and civic welfare (eight-deity assembly including the judicial triad of 包公, 法主圣君, and 二郎神, plus 孔子, 财神爷, 保生大帝, 济公, and 太岁, right). The back hall elevates Guanyin to 佛祖 (Ancestor Buddha) status and positions her as the soteriological anchor of the entire compound — a Buddhist transcendent frame behind the Taoist-folk institutional identity, directly illustrating the Buddhist-folk syncretic dynamic Li Ying's EASSSR paper investigates. New grammar contributions include *frames[ecomorphic]* as a relation value, aspirational naming as a fourth naming convention, and the Tiger God cave as the corpus's first ecomorphic altar format. The temple introduces seven deity types first documented in this corpus, with Judge Bao's judicial assembly constituting the most elaborate divine-justice programme in the corpus.

